{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789235016,"nonce":"VZpG+m5wjtkHFT9fBTgWk/C5qms=","title":"Integer-typed arithmetic for loop counters and accumulators","message":"bench/ash method_call, free_call and closure_call now run at 7.5 ns per iteration with everything inlined and every loop-carried value carried as raw f64. The remaining cost is the float dependency chain: sum = (acc * 31 + (i % 8)) % 4294967296 is fmul, fadd, and two remainder sequences (fmul, frintz, fmul, fsub, copysign each), about 25 cycles of latency. Ash runs the same kernel at 1.5 ns because its arithmetic is integer-typed.\n\nWren has only doubles, so the equivalent is a range/integrality analysis: a loop-carried Num that starts integral, is only updated by integer-preserving ops (add/sub/mul of integral values, remainder, bit ops, floor/truncate) and stays within +-2^53 can be carried as i64, with imul/iadd/and (for power-of-two remainders, plus sign fix-up) and a single conversion at the loop exit. Comparisons against integral bounds stay in the integer domain. Overflow guards fall back to the float form. Expected: the three call kernels near 2 ns per iteration; mandelbrot's iteration counter and pixel loops too.","files":null}]}